Phase 1: Foundation

This phase prepares the host PC for the lab: a hypervisor, an isolated lab network, the installer images, and this repository. No virtual machines are built yet; that starts in Phase 2.

What this phase covers
Checking the host can run virtual machines
Planning where the VMs and installer images are stored
Downloading the operating system images
Installing VMware Workstation Pro
Creating an isolated lab network with a fixed addressing plan
Creating the GitHub repository for documentation and detections
Host hardware

The lab runs on my everyday desktop, so it is sized to fit alongside normal use.

Component	Detail
CPU	Intel Core i5-8400 (6 cores)
RAM	16 GB (2 of 4 slots used)
Storage	SSD (system drive) and a 1.8 TB HDD
Host OS	Windows 11 Home
Design decisions
Decision	Choice	Why
Hypervisor	VMware Workstation Pro	Free, and its virtual network editor is simple to work with
SIEM hosting	Self-managed Elastic Stack	Free on the Basic license, and building it is part of the learning
Network type	NAT	VMs can reach the internet for updates, but devices on my home network cannot reach the VMs
Elastic VM storage	SSD	Search speed depends on disk speed
Windows VM and ISO storage	HDD	They need space more than speed
Ubuntu version	24.04 LTS	The newest LTS (26.04) was not yet on Elastic's support matrix when I built this

RAM is the tightest resource. The planned allocation is 4 GB for the Elastic VM, 2 to 3 GB for the domain controller, and 3 to 4 GB for the Windows workstation, with the rest left for the host. The lab is suspended when I use the PC for anything heavy.

Step 1: Pre-flight checks
Open Task Manager and go to the Performance tab.
Under CPU, confirm that "Virtualization" shows "Enabled". If it shows "Disabled", turn on Intel VT-x (or AMD-V) in the BIOS before continuing.
Under Memory, note how many slots are in use, in case of a later upgrade.
Under each disk, note whether it is an SSD or HDD and how much space is free.
Open Settings > System > About and note the Windows edition.
Step 2: Create the lab folders
Folder	Drive	Contents
Lab\VMs	SSD	The Elastic VM
Lab\VMs	HDD	The Windows VMs
Lab\ISOs	HDD	Installer images

I freed up the SSD to about 60 to 70 GB so the Elastic VM has room to grow.

Step 3: Download the installer images

All three are saved to Lab\ISOs.

Image	Used for	Source
Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS	Elastic VM (Phase 2)	https://ubuntu.com/download/server (under previous releases) or https://releases.ubuntu.com/24.04/
Windows Server evaluation	Domain controller (Phase 3)	https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/evalcenter/download-windows-server-2025
Windows 11 Enterprise evaluation	Workstation (Phase 3)	https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/evalcenter/download-windows-11-enterprise

Notes:

The Windows images are free, time-limited evaluations, which is fine for a lab. Microsoft may ask for a short registration form.
The main Ubuntu download button offers the newest release. Scroll down for 24.04 LTS.

To confirm a download is intact, compare its hash with the value published by the vendor:

powershell
Get-FileHash "path\to\file.iso" -Algorithm SHA256
Step 4: Install VMware Workstation Pro
Create a free account at https://support.broadcom.com and sign in.
Go to the downloads area and find VMware Workstation Pro for Windows.
Choose the newest release. I used 26H1u1.
If the download icon shows "Screening Required", tick the terms checkbox, click the icon, and complete the one-time export compliance form. Then click the icon again.
Check the installer's hash against the SHA2 value shown on the download page, using the Get-FileHash command above.
Run the installer with the default options and restart if prompted.

No license key is needed.

Then set where new VMs are stored: Edit > Preferences > Workspace, and set the default location to Lab\VMs on the HDD. The Elastic VM is placed on the SSD by hand when it is created.

Step 5: Build the lab network
In VMware, open Edit > Virtual Network Editor.
Click "Change Settings" and approve the administrator prompt.
Select the VMnet8 row (type NAT).
Set Subnet IP to 10.10.10.0 and Subnet mask to 255.255.255.0.
Click "NAT Settings" and confirm the Gateway IP is 10.10.10.2.
Click "DHCP Settings" and set the range to 10.10.10.100 through 10.10.10.200.
Click Apply, then OK.

The DHCP range is kept away from the low addresses so the servers can use fixed ones.

Addressing plan
Host	Address
Host PC (VMnet8 adapter)	10.10.10.1
Gateway and DNS (VMware NAT)	10.10.10.2
Elastic VM	10.10.10.10
Domain controller	10.10.10.20
Windows workstation	10.10.10.30
DHCP pool	10.10.10.100 to 10.10.10.200
Verify

Run ipconfig in PowerShell on the host. The adapter named "VMware Network Adapter VMnet8" should show:

IPv4 Address. . . . . . . . . . . : 10.10.10.1
Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.255.0
Default Gateway . . . . . . . . . :

The empty default gateway is expected. The host uses its normal connection for internet access; only the VMs use 10.10.10.2.

